# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: epictetus-4.logic.r1 and epictetus-4.logic.r1.2 have "assent_in_power → ¬anxious_assent_warranted"; epictetus-4.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it; they differ when only "not assenting to the false and not turning from the truth is in one's own power" and "there is reason to be anxious about assent and truth" are true, which the second allows
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps epictetus-4.logic.r1 and epictetus-4.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "not assenting to the false and not turning from the truth is in one's own power" and "there is reason to be anxious about assent and truth" are true. Is S5, "If a man be of such a good disposition as to be anxious about these things I will remind him of this: Why are you anxious?", true then?
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/readings: S9 is read 2 ways: epictetus-4.logic.r1 and epictetus-4.logic.r1.3 have "□(¬rule → fail)"; epictetus-4.logic.r1.2 has "¬rule → □fail"; they differ when only "necessarily, (if it is not so that one desires nothing which belongs to others and tries to avoid none of the things which are not in one's power, then one fails in one's desires and falls into that which one would avoid)" is true, which the first allows
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/answered: S9: answered yes, which keeps epictetus-4.logic.r1 and epictetus-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "necessarily, (if it is not so that one desires nothing which belongs to others and tries to avoid none of the things which are not in one's power, then one fails in one's desires and falls into that which one would avoid)" and "necessarily, (if it is not so that one desires nothing which belongs to others and tries to avoid none of the things which are not in one's power, then one fails in one's desires and falls into that which one would avoid)" are true and "one desires nothing which belongs to others and tries to avoid none of the things which are not in one's power" and "necessarily, one fails in one's desires and falls into that which one would avoid" are false. Is S9, "If you do not observe this rule, you must of necessity fail in your desires and fall into that which you would avoid.", true then?
epictetus-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: epictetus-4.logic.r1 has "rule → ¬concern_external" and "¬choose_fail"; epictetus-4.logic.r1.2 has "rule → ¬concern_external"; they differ when only "one is turning oneself to things which are not within the power of the will" and "one chooses to desire that which one will fail to obtain and to attempt to avoid that into which one will fall" are true, which the second allows; epictetus-4.logic.r1.3 has "assent_in_power → ¬anxious_assent_warranted" and "rule → ¬concern_external"
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: epictetus-4.logic.r1 has "¬worry"; epictetus-4.logic.r1.2 and epictetus-4.logic.r1.3 have "rule → ¬worry"; they differ when only "one says "What shall I do? how will it be? how will it turn out? will this happen?", i.e. there is room for such anxious words" is true, which the second allows
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps epictetus-4.logic.r1, to: Suppose "one says "What shall I do? how will it be? how will it turn out? will this happen?", i.e. there is room for such anxious words" is true and "one desires nothing which belongs to others and tries to avoid none of the things which are not in one's power" is false. Is the conclusion, "What is the difficulty here? where is there room for the words How will it be? and How will it turn out? and Will this happen or that?", true then?
epictetus-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: epictetus-4.logic.r1 takes for granted "it is not so that one chooses to desire that which one will fail to obtain and to attempt to avoid that into which one will fall"; epictetus-4.logic.r1.2 and epictetus-4.logic.r1.3 do not
epictetus-4.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps epictetus-4.logic.r1, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "it is not so that one chooses to desire that which one will fail to obtain and to attempt to avoid that into which one will fall"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/unfaithful: epictetus-4.logic.r1 P4 (S5): the sentence has no word of "not assenting to the false and not turning from the truth is in one's own power": is it the sentence P4 came from?
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/unfaithful: epictetus-4.logic.r1.2 P4 (S5): the sentence has no word of "not assenting to the false and not turning from the truth is in one's own power": is it the sentence P4 came from?
